INT. SHIPPING CONTAINER ON DRIFTING ICE FLOE - DAY

Gray light through a rust hole. Three bedrolls, a camp stove, a radio with its back off. Stillness, finally.

HALVOR NILSEN, 50s, sits on an overturned bucket. DEE ARMSTRONG, 30s, tends the stove. PASHA VOLKOV, 20s, lies on a bedroll, eyes closed, a dark stain on his sleeve.

HALVOR
(to the container)
The wind's dropped.

DEE
Finally.

PASHA
Tell me it's warm out there.

HALVOR
It's not warm. It's quiet.

DEE
Quiet's the same as warm, right now.

PASHA
Dee, did you boil the rest of the coffee?

DEE
I boiled all of it. We're out.

PASHA
Out of coffee.

DEE
Out of coffee.

HALVOR
Out of coffee, out of fuel in two days, out of floe in three.

DEE ^
Halvor.

HALVOR
I'm just saying the weather's good for talking.

PASHA
Then talk.

HALVOR
The floe split in the night. The crack's under the east wall. I heard it.

DEE
You said the wind --

HALVOR
The wind dropped. The ice didn't.

PASHA
(very quietly)
How long?

HALVOR
Hours. Maybe less.

DEE
(to no one)
Of course it did.

PASHA
Open the door, Halvor.

HALVOR
Pasha --

PASHA
I want to see it.

Halvor stands. The container groans, a long slow note like a cello, and the three of them look at the floor.
